# indox-client

`indox-client` is a lightweight Python SDK that makes it easy to call the Indox media and
document conversion microservices. The library wraps the public HTTPS APIs that live in
the `media` and `docs_indox_data_scaler` projects and automatically sends the API key that
users generate inside the Indox dashboard.

> **Reminder:** OAuth logins are still handled by `indox-backend`. This package is only a
> network client – it does not issue tokens.

## Features

- Simple `DocsClient` and `MediaClient` wrappers for `/api/v1/docs/*`, `/api/v1/image/*`,
  `/api/v1/video/*`, conversion status, and masked download endpoints.
- Handles `Authorization: Bearer <API key>` headers and propagates retries / timeouts in
  one place.
- Works with API keys created through `POST /api/v1/auth/api-keys/` in the backend.
- Gives you a single `IndoxClient` facade so your app code does not care which hostname
  serves each service.

## Installation

```bash
pip install indox-client
```

The project is pure Python and only depends on `requests`.

## Getting an API key

Every user can mint their own API key via the Indox dashboard or directly through the
backend REST API:

```
POST https://dev-api.indox.org/api/v1/auth/api-keys/
Authorization: Bearer <your normal OIDC access token>
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "automation-bot",
  "permissions": ["read", "write"],
  "expires_in_days": 30,
  "rate_limit_per_hour": 500
}
```

The plain-text key is returned only once and always starts with `ak_`. Copy it to your
password manager and feed it into this SDK:

```bash
export INDOX_API_KEY="ak_live_xxxxxx"
```

## Quick start

```python
from indox_client import IndoxClient

client = IndoxClient(
    api_key="ak_live_xxxxxx",
    docs_base_url="https://dev-docs-api.indox.org",
    media_base_url="https://dev-media-api.indox.org",
)

# Convert a DOCX file to PDF + HTML with the docs service
job = client.docs.convert_file(
    file_path="proposal.docx",
    target_formats=["pdf", "html"],
)

print("Job queued:", job["job_id"])

# Kick off an image conversion
image_job = client.media.convert_image(
    file_path="logo.png",
    target_formats=["webp", "avif"],
)
print("Image conversion id:", image_job["id"])
```

## Docs client highlights

```python
# Discover what inputs/outputs are allowed before converting
formats = client.docs.supported_conversions()
print(formats["operations"][0])

# Convert a remote file without uploading
resp = client.docs.convert_url(
    file_url="https://example.com/manual.md",
    target_formats=["pdf"],
    destination="aws",
    options={"watermark": {"text": "Draft", "opacity": 0.2}},
)

# Poll the latest status
status = client.docs.get_conversion(resp["job_id"])

# Download the final artifact
client.docs.download(conversion_id=resp["job_id"], output_path="manual.pdf", fmt="PDF")
```

Behind the scenes the SDK calls:

- `POST /api/v1/docs/convert/` for multipart uploads
- `POST /api/v1/docs/convert/json/` when you pass `file_url` or `s3_key`
- `GET /api/v1/conversion/{id}/` for status
- `GET /api/v1/docs/{id}/download/` for downloads

## Media client highlights

```python
# Inspect available image/video formats and operations once at startup
image_formats = client.media.image_formats()
video_formats = client.media.video_formats()
print("Image formats:", image_formats["supported_outputs"])

# Image conversion
img = client.media.convert_image("portrait.heic", ["jpeg", "png"], destination="aws")

# Video conversion with a remote URL
vid = client.media.convert_video(
    file_url="https://cdn.example.com/sample.mov",
    target_formats=["mp4"],
    destination="google_drive",
    options={"quality": "medium"},
)

# Status + download
info = client.media.get_image_conversion(img["id"])
client.media.download_media(conversion_id=img["id"], output_path="portrait.jpg")
```

Endpoints covered:

- `POST /api/v1/image/convert/`
- `POST /api/v1/video/convert/`
- `GET /api/v1/image/conversion/{id}/`
- `GET /api/v1/video/conversion/{id}/`
- `GET /api/v1/media/{id}/download/`

## Working with environments

Most teams deploy separate hosts for every stage. Pass the correct base URLs when you
instantiate the client or pick them up from the environment:

```python
import os
from indox_client import IndoxClient

client = IndoxClient.from_env(
    docs_base_url=os.getenv("INDOX_DOCS_URL", "https://dev-docs-api.indox.org"),
    media_base_url=os.getenv("INDOX_MEDIA_URL", "https://dev-media-api.indox.org"),
)
```

## Error handling

All network errors raise a subclass of `IndoxClientError`. HTTP errors expose the
response body, status code, and request id (if present) so you can log/alert on them.

```python
from indox_client import IndoxClientError

try:
    client.docs.convert_file("bad.xyz", ["pdf"])
except IndoxClientError as exc:
    print(exc.status_code, exc.detail)
```

